  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 404 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nShowers with isolated embedded lightning may linger into Monday.\nHow long these showers linger and therefore where the effective\nboundary sets up will be what sets the stage for the next round\nof potential severe storms Monday afternoon/evening. Provided\nclouds clear efficiently into the afternoon hours, an additional\nHeat Advisory may be needed Monday afternoon for heat index\nvalues in the low 100s and temperatures in the upper 80s to low\n90s once again, as well as enhanced humidity (dewpoints in the\n70s advecting in as well as any additional moisture from storms\nSunday night/Monday morning). Depending on the westward extent\nof cloud cover, may only need portions of southwestern Wisconsin\nunder a Heat Advisory, and conditions will continue to be\nevaluated as the event draws closer.\n\nMonday evening, a shortwave ejects from Canada, riding over the\nridging that remains across the Upper Midwest and attaching\nitself to any boundary lingering from the morning convection.\nExpecting CAPE values to recover quickly into the 2000 to 3000\nJ/kg range after cloud cover exits, and for shear to increase to\n40-45 kt ahead of the shortwave/effective cold front. Severe\nstorms once again become possible as the shortwave moves through\nthe region, but will depend largely on precursory conditions.\nTherefore, confidence is rising in timing, but impacts remain in\nquestion.\n\nMonday night, storms finally exit to the east and northwesterly\nwinds develop, bringing in cooler and drier air with lows in the\nmid 60s. Tuesday, expect northerly winds to continue as high\npressure builds in, continuing to reduce the humidity but\nkeeping highs in the mid-80s away from Lake Michigan (lake\nbreeze influence). Dry conditions under high pressure linger\nthrough at least Wednesday, but high pressure begins to break\ndown Thursday night into Friday ahead of low pressure ejecting \nfrom the northern Plains.\n\nNext chances for storms develop Thursday night through Friday \nas warm frontogenesis builds across the Upper Midwest and low\npressure makes its closest approach. Precipitation chances\ndiminish into next weekend as high pressure centered over\nnorthern Ontario begins to build back southward.\n\nMH",
